ck and listen I've  done it I didn't find it fun I'm not I  know a lot of people like him sir I'm  not sure they find it fun a lot of the  dialogue is pretty boring the fifth  point they make is that they focus on  the most common words and phrases I'm  not a great believer in this great  emphasis on the most common words and  phrases I find the most common words and  phrases well because they are the most  common and they'll show up all the time  no matter what content you're working  with but that ultimately if your goal is  to be able to speak on a variety of  subjects you need a lot of words the  bigger biggest I would say that this  advantage of Pimsleur is that you learn  so few words sixth advantage they claim  is that there's no rote memory which is  fine that you learn things naturally and  I think this is true if you spend a lot  of time let's think you're gonna start  picking up on things  however they they basically say  flashcards don't work I find that  flashcards and a number of other parts  of the traditional teaching methodology  are things that help you notice  including reviewing the out grammar rule  certainly they get a general sense of  the language to start with and then  later on from time to time to help you  notice things that you just won't notice  otherwise the seventh thing they say is  that the way they teach you can learn to  speak without an accent people will  mistake you for a native that's simply  nonsense in ten days of this system you  are not going to sound like a native my  own preference in fact is to wait until  I've had enough exposure to the language  before I even start to pronounce the  language but that's individual it  depends on your personal goals it  depends on where you are if you're  located where the language is spoken you  have to get cracking speaking the  language more quickly the eighth thing  they mention is that they've done 80  years of 50 sorry 50 years of research I  don't believe they've been doing any  